Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Test Bench Nine Pulls Past the Barline
- Story & Context: The debut runs at 112 BPM. Matei opens with a clipped kick-snare pattern and closed sixteenth hats while Irina plays one deadened clavinet fourth. Ana begins a buhai DRAW just after beat two and continues it across the next barline, refusing the obvious one-bar phrase. Luc keeps a two-note muted bass ostinato steady underneath. Midway through the second bar Ana's friction becomes irregular and enters BUCKLE; Matei removes the next snare instead of accenting it, leaving the rough horsehair chatter exposed. Solène answers only after DROP with one bass-saxophone minor-second fall. In the middle section the band repeats the same groove three times while Ana produces three different pull lengths, proving that the acoustic contour, not the sequence, determines the perceived form. The last pull ends a half-beat earlier than expected and the entire ensemble cuts with it.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Low-angle view of a green machinery test bench marked 9 beside a buhai, a paper 4/4 grid showing one thick friction line crossing directly through the barline, wet horsehair catching fluorescent light. Sparse spoken fragments limited to “bench nine,” “keep pulling,” “past the line,” and “drop early.”

Track 2: Moisture Reading Makes the Groove Stutter
- Story & Context: At 118 BPM, Ana alternates smooth DRAW gestures with deliberately drier pulls that break rapidly into BUCKLE. The rhythm section never changes tempo. Irina's clavinet continues straight sixteenths during smooth pulls but removes every third attack during rough ones. Matei keeps kick steady and uses one talancă-style bell hit at the first clean DROP after each unstable passage. Luc removes electric bass for the driest pull, revealing that the buhai can dominate the low register without giving the listener a conventional root sequence. Solène uses short breathy bass-saxophone stabs between friction bursts. The central breakdown contains no drum fill: Ana wets her hands again in audible room silence, begins one perfectly smooth pull, and the full groove simply re-enters beneath it.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Galvanized wash basin, handwritten moisture gauge, and horsehair bundle above oil-dark concrete; two waveform sketches labeled SMOOTH and BUCKLE with the second breaking into irregular blocks. Spoken fragments limited to “too dry,” “catch and slip,” “wet again,” and “groove returns.”

Track 3: The Furrow Ends Before the Motor Stops
- Story & Context: The closer runs at 106 BPM and uses the most open arrangement. Luc plays a muted electric-bass root only on selected offbeats. Matei reduces the kit to kick, rim, and one closed hat. Irina plays clipped suspended-second clavinet dyads every two bars. Ana performs long low pulls separated by unusually large DROP silences. Solène holds no sustained notes; she answers each pull with one short bass-saxophone fundamental after the acoustic bellow has completely died. In the final minute, the machine-like rhythm continues while Ana progressively shortens each friction gesture: two bars, one bar, three beats, two beats, then a final half-beat scrape. After the last DROP, the rhythm section continues alone for four measures, as if an agricultural motor keeps turning after the plough has already left the soil. The final stop is dry and unanimous.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Empty test hall with a long painted furrow line ending several meters before a stationary electric motor and belt guard, buhai resting upright beside the line, four final bars drawn beyond the last friction mark. Sparse spoken fragments limited to “furrow ends,” “motor stays,” “four more measures,” and “cut dry.”

